  "context": "First published in 1865, this text forms an essential part of Karl Marx's critique of political economy. It provides simplified, yet rigorous, explanations of key capitalist economic concepts.",
  "relatesToToday": "Directly refutes corporate claims that raising minimum wages or granting employee pay raises inevitably drives inflation. It equips workers and trade union negotiators with the economic proof that wage increases come out of bloated corporate profits, not consumers' pockets.",
  "keyTakeaways": [
    "The value of a commodity is determined by the total quantity of socially necessary labor time required for its production.",
    "Labor-power is a commodity itself, whose value is determined by the cost of maintaining the worker and their family.",
    "Capitalists purchase labor-power to extract surplus value, which forms the basis of profit, rent, and interest."
